  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How does a plant know it is being eaten?",
      "a": "Plants detect torn cells and chemicals in insect saliva. This triggers hormone signals, such as jasmonic acid, that spread through the plant and switch on its defences.",
      "asks": [
        "Can plants feel insects eating them?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What chemicals do plants use for defence?",
      "a": "Many: bitter tannins, nicotine in tobacco, caffeine in coffee and tea, mustard oils in cabbages and the burning capsaicin in chilli peppers. Many flavours we enjoy started as plant defences.",
      "asks": [
        "Why is chilli hot?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Can plants call for help?",
      "a": "Yes, in a way. Maize and other plants attacked by caterpillars release scents that attract parasitic wasps, which lay their eggs inside the caterpillars.",
      "asks": [
        "How do plants use wasps?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Do plants warn each other?",
      "a": "Plants can pick up the alarm scents of an attacked neighbour and start preparing defences before insects reach them. Scientists are still studying how common and how strong this effect is.",
      "asks": [
        "Can plants talk to each other?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What physical defences do plants have?",
      "a": "Thorns, spines, stinging hairs like a nettle's, sticky hairs that trap insects, and tough or waxy leaves. Holly even grows pricklier leaves low down, where animals graze.",
      "asks": [
        "Why do plants have thorns?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "A 1990 study showed that maize seedlings attacked by caterpillars release scents that attract parasitic wasps.",
    "Jasmonic acid is a plant hormone that switches on defences after insect attack.",
    "Holly trees tend to grow spiny leaves low down, within reach of grazing animals, and smoother leaves higher up."
  ],
